# CODEOWNERS — required reviewers for trstctl (TEST-006)
#
# GitHub requests a review from the listed owner(s) on any PR that touches a
# matching path, and — when "Require review from Code Owners" is enabled in branch
# protection (see docs/branch-protection.md) — a PR cannot merge without their
# approval. The last matching pattern wins, so the security-critical blocks at the
# bottom override the catch-all above.
#
# This file is the in-repo, reviewable record of *who must sign off on what*. The
# audit (TEST-006) flagged that ownership of the root-of-trust paths was not
# codified; codifying it here makes the gate provable from the artifact, and
# docs/codeowners_test.go asserts every security-critical path below stays covered.
#
# Owner: @imfeelingtheagi is the sole maintainer. This file previously named a
# @ctlplne/security TEAM that does not exist on GitHub, which made every rule
# below unresolvable — and, with require_code_owner_reviews enabled, made main
# unmergeable. Ownership now names a real account.
#
# `ctlplne` remains the canonical namespace for clone URLs and container images
# (github.com/ctlplne/trstctl, ghcr.io/ctlplne/trstctl). It is NOT the module
# namespace: the Go module paths are vanity paths under trstctl.com and
# deliberately do not match the code host — see the Install section of
# clients/sdk/README.md for the go-import hosting that needs.
#
# What this file still buys a single maintainer: GitHub auto-requests review on
# matching paths, so a change to the root of trust announces itself in the PR
# rather than relying on the author noticing. The coverage test asserts every
# security-critical path below resolves to a non-empty owner.

# Default owner for everything not matched more specifically below.
*                               @imfeelingtheagi

# --- Security-critical: the root of trust (TEST-006) ------------------------
# A compromise of any of these is, per README.md AN-4, "the company is over." They get
# explicit, mandatory ownership so no change lands without a security review.

# AN-3 — the sole cryptography boundary. Nothing else may import crypto/*.
/internal/crypto/                @imfeelingtheagi

# AN-4 — the isolated signing service (private-key operations) and its gRPC wire
# contract (internal/signing/proto — the boundary to the most sensitive process).
/internal/signing/               @imfeelingtheagi
/internal/signing/proto/         @imfeelingtheagi
/cmd/trstctl-signer/            @imfeelingtheagi

# AN-1 — multi-tenant storage, RLS policies, and migrations.
/internal/store/                 @imfeelingtheagi

# The architecture linter that enforces AN-1, AN-2, AN-3, AN-5, AN-8 and AN-9 in CI
# — weakening it silently weakens every guardrail, so it is owned alongside the
# crypto/signer/store. AN-4, AN-6 and AN-7 have no analyzer; see MAINTAINERS.md.
/tools/trstctllint/             @imfeelingtheagi

# --- Security & supply-chain process surfaces -------------------------------
# CI/release pipelines, the security policy, and the disclosure docs gate what ships
# and how, so they carry explicit ownership too.
/.github/                        @imfeelingtheagi
/.github/workflows/              @imfeelingtheagi
/.github/CODEOWNERS              @imfeelingtheagi
/SECURITY.md                     @imfeelingtheagi
/deploy/                         @imfeelingtheagi
